--[[
`debug.log` provides leveled logging with checked format strings. A filtered
severity call with a literal format in statement position evaluates none of its
arguments.

```nupp:fragment
debug.log.error("cannot open %s: %s", path, reason)
debug.log.warn("retrying in %dms", delay)
debug.log.info("loaded %d entities", count)
debug.log.debug("state=%s", state)
```

Each severity accepts a `string.format` directive string and the arguments it
calls for. The directives and their argument types are checked where the call is
written, by the same machinery that checks `string.format`, so a missing
argument or a `%d` handed a string is a compile error rather than a line that
fails at run time.

```text
debug.log.error("id %d")
  error: NUPP2006: omitted argument 2 supplies nil, not number
```

Because `%s` accepts anything, nothing needs `tostring` and a nil argument
prints as `nil` rather than raising. Lines go to standard error until a host
says otherwise.

`%?` is Nupp's debug directive. It requires `types.Debug`, calls the value's
`debug()` method, and formats the returned string as `%s`:

```nupp:fragment
debug.log.debug("state=%?", state)
```

At lowered call sites that method call is inside the level guard, so filtered
logs do not render the value. Named loggers perform the same check in their
already-lazy writer.

## Severity calls are intrinsics

A severity call in statement position whose format is a literal is lowered
rather than called:

```nupp:fragment
debug.log.error("id %d", id)
```

```lua
local __nuppModule = require("debug.log").formodule("amb")
if __nuppModule.on[1] then __nuppModule.emit(1,47,string.format("id %d",id)) end
```

Three properties follow from that shape, and together they are the reason this
is a compiler intrinsic rather than a library.

### Filtered calls evaluate nothing

The level test stands at the call site, so the arguments of a suppressed line
are never computed. `debug.log.debug("%s", render(state))` does not call `render`
when debug is off. No library can decline to evaluate its own arguments.

### Module name and line are constants

The compiler is generating the file, so it writes both in directly: the module
once in the prologue, the line at each site. Nothing is recovered at run time,
nothing depends on the `debug` library, and there is no per-module boilerplate
to write or to keep in step with a rename.

### Filtered call cost

A filtered call costs an upvalue read, an array index and a branch. The view is
bound once per module, and `on` is an array indexed by severity, shared with
every other module so a level change is seen everywhere at once.

### Forms that stay calls

Every other form keeps an ordinary call meaning exactly the same thing, only
slower. The module shows as `?`, and the arguments are evaluated:

| Form | Lowered | Reason |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `debug.log.info("id %d", id)` | yes | |
| `debug.log.info(format, id)` | no | the format is not a literal |
| `local f = debug.log.info` | no | a value, not a call |
| `x = debug.log.info("hi")` | no | not statement position |
| `local debug = ...` | no | a local shadow of the compiler-provided `debug` |
| `logger:info("id %d", id)` | no | a named logger, not the path |

## Levels

`level` reads the threshold and `setlevel` moves it:

```nupp
debug.log.setlevel("debug") -- returns the previous one
debug.log.level() -- "debug"
```

The five levels are `"off"`, `"error"`, `"warn"`, `"info"` and `"debug"`, each
admitting itself and everything above it, so `"warn"` emits warnings and errors.
The default is `"warn"`.

The parameter is that literal union, so a string from outside the program has to
be narrowed to one of the five before it can be passed.
`os.getenv("LOG_LEVEL") or "warn"` is `string`, and `string` is not one of them:

```nupp
local wanted = os.getenv("LOG_LEVEL")
if wanted == "debug" or wanted == "info" or wanted == "warn" then
    debug.log.setlevel(wanted)
end
```

A level that is not one of the five is a compile error where it is a literal and
an ordinary raise where it is not.

`debug.log.enabled(level)` returns whether a level would emit. Use it to guard
preparation spanning more than one call, which no single lowered site can elide:

```nupp:fragment
if debug.log.enabled("debug") then
    local report = summarize(world)
    debug.log.debug("world: %s", report)
end
```

### Module levels

`setmodulelevel` overrides the threshold for one exact module name. Modules
without an override inherit the global threshold, including after it changes:

```nupp
debug.log.setlevel("warn")
debug.log.setmodulelevel("game.physics", "debug")
```

The override reaches existing and future module views. `modulelevel` reads the
effective threshold, and passing `"inherit"` removes the override:

```nupp
debug.log.modulelevel("game.physics") -- "debug"
debug.log.setmodulelevel("game.physics", "inherit") -- restore the global threshold
```

Names are exact: configuring `game.physics` does not also configure
`game.physics.collision`. A named logger with the same name uses the same
threshold.

## Swapping the back end

A host that logs through its own facility installs a callback sink. It receives
the parts of each line and does not pay for formatting it discards:

```nupp:fragment
local _ = debug.log.setsink(debug.log.callback(function(level: integer, module: string, line: integer, message: string): nil
    sdl.logMessage(CATEGORY, PRIORITY[level], ("%s:%d %s"):format(module, line, message))
end))
```

`level` is `1` error, `2` warn, `3` info, `4` debug; `debug.log.levelname` turns
one back into its name. `line` is `0` for a line the compiler could not
attribute, which is every line from a named logger.

`writersink` adapts an [](io.Writer), using the built-in rendering or a supplied
formatter. The sink takes and closes the writer. A borrowed writer must remain
valid until the sink is replaced and closed.

```nupp
local file = assert(io.open("game.log", "a"))
local previous = debug.log.setsink(debug.log.writersink(io.Writer.of(file)))
local _ = debug.log.setsink(previous)
```

`setsink` takes the new sink and returns the previous one. Bind the returned
sink to restore it later, or bind it to `_` to close it. The default sink writes
to standard error. A formatter belongs to its writer sink:

```nupp
local file = assert(io.open("game.log", "a"))
local previous = debug.log.setsink(debug.log.writersink(io.Writer.of(file), function(level: integer, module: string, line: integer, message: string, stamp: string): string
    return ("%s[%s] %s"):format(stamp, debug.log.levelname(level), message)
end))
local _ = debug.log.setsink(previous)
```

## Timestamps

`debug.log.timestamp()` returns the current time formatted, recomputed at most
once per wall-clock second and shared by every logger. It is a pull rather than
something pushed to sinks, so a host that stamps its own lines never pays for
one.

```nupp
debug.log.settimestampformat("%H:%M:%S ") -- returns the previous format
debug.log.settimestampformat("") -- off
debug.log.timestampformat() -- ""
```

## Named loggers

`named` returns a logger carrying a fixed name, with a method per severity:

```nupp:fragment
local physics = debug.log.named("physics")
physics:warn("step %d took %.2fms", step, elapsed)
```

Use one for a subsystem that does not correspond to a module, and for a call
site the intrinsic cannot reach. Repeating a name returns the same logger. Their
methods are replaced when the level or target changes, so a filtered call
reaches an empty function rather than a test, but the arguments are still
evaluated, which is the cost of a name chosen at run time.

## Cost

Logging is initialized at startup to provide the global `warn` function. A
module that uses the severity calls also binds its own logging view.
]]
module debug.log

--- The threshold, from silent to most verbose. Each level admits itself and
--- everything above it, so "warn" emits warnings and errors.
export type Level = "off" | "error" | "warn" | "info" | "debug"

--- A module threshold or the instruction to resume inheriting the global one.
export type LevelOverride = Level | "inherit"

--- A level as a sink sees it: 1 error, 2 warn, 3 info, 4 debug.
export type Severity = 1 | 2 | 3 | 4

--- Receives the severity, source, and message of an emitted line.
---
--- Replacing this replaces the back end, so a host logging through its own
--- facility pays for nothing it discards. No timestamp is passed, because a sink
--- that wants one asks.
export type SinkCallback = function(level: Severity, module: string, line: integer, message: string): nil

--- Renders a line for a writer sink.
export type Formatter = function(level: Severity, module: string, line: integer, message: string, stamp: string): string

--- An owned destination for emitted log messages.
export sealed interface Sink is own.Closeable
    --- Emits a message at its severity and source location.
    emit: function(exclusive self: Sink, level: Severity, module: string, line: integer, message: string): nil
end

--- A logger carrying a fixed name, for subsystems and for call sites the intrinsic
--- cannot rewrite. Changing the level or target restamps every logger, so a filtered
--- call reaches an empty function rather than a test.
export record Logger
    --- The name every line from this logger carries.
    @readonly name: string

    --- Logs at debug. Accepts `string.format` directives.
    debug: function<F is string>(self: Logger, fmt: F, ...: unpackof __NuppFormatArguments(F, types.Debug)): nil

    --- Logs at info. Accepts `string.format` directives.
    info: function<F is string>(self: Logger, fmt: F, ...: unpackof __NuppFormatArguments(F, types.Debug)): nil

    --- Logs at warn. Accepts `string.format` directives.
    warn: function<F is string>(self: Logger, fmt: F, ...: unpackof __NuppFormatArguments(F, types.Debug)): nil

    --- Logs at error. Accepts `string.format` directives.
    error: function<F is string>(self: Logger, fmt: F, ...: unpackof __NuppFormatArguments(F, types.Debug)): nil

    --- Whether this logger would emit at `level`.
    enabled: function(self: Logger, level: Level): boolean
end

local NAMES = {"error", "warn", "info", "debug",}
local INDEX = {off = 0, error = 1, warn = 2, info = 3, debug = 4,}

local hasffi, ffi = pcall(require, "ffi")
if hasffi then
    pcall(ffi.cdef, "typedef int64_t nupp_time_t; nupp_time_t time(nupp_time_t *t);")
end

-- `os.time` is NYI in LuaJIT and stitches the trace it stands on, so the second
-- is read through the FFI. The drift check is what keeps a resolved-but-wrong-
-- width `time` from answering nonsense: some Windows CRTs inline it to `_time64`
-- or give it a 32-bit `time_t`, and neither fails to resolve.
local clock: any = os.time
if hasffi then
    local resolved, fn = pcall(function(): any
        return ffi.C.time
    end)
    if resolved and fn then
        local null = ffi.cast("nupp_time_t*", 0)
        local valid, answered = pcall(fn, null)
        local drift = valid and ((tonumber(answered) or 0) - os.time()) or math.huge
        if drift > -2 and drift < 2 then
            clock = function(): number
                return tonumber(fn(null)) or 0
            end
        end
    end
end

-- Exported functions are bound across the whole file, so state that shares a name
-- with one (`formatter`, the warning switch) is named apart from it here, and the
-- severity functions, which would shadow Lua's `error` and `debug`, are bound at the
-- end.
local threshold: integer = 2
local target: any = nil
local datefmt = "%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S "
local lasttick: any = -1
local laststamp = ""
local lastfmt = ""
local registry: any = {}
local views: any = {}
local scoped: any = {}
local overrides: any = {}

--- The global level flags a generated module view shares. Lowering reaches them;
--- nothing written by hand needs to.
--- @internal
export const on = {false, false, false, false,}

local function seton(flags: {boolean}, index: integer): nil
    for i = 1, 4 do
        flags[i] = i <= index
    end
end

local function admit(index: integer): nil
    seton(on, index)
    threshold = index
    for name, flags in pairs(scoped) do
        if overrides[name] == nil then
            seton(flags, index)
        end
    end
end

admit(threshold)

local function stamp(): string
    if datefmt == "" then
        return ""
    end
    local tick = clock()
    if tick ~= lasttick or datefmt ~= lastfmt then
        lasttick = tick
        lastfmt = datefmt
        laststamp = os.date(datefmt) as string
    end

    return laststamp
end

local function renderline(
    severity: Severity,
    module: string,
    line: integer,
    message: string,
    formatter: Formatter?
): string
    local at = stamp()
    if formatter then
        return formatter(severity, module, line, message, at) .. "\n"
    end
    if line > 0 then
        return at .. NAMES[severity] .. " " .. module .. ":" .. line .. ": " .. message .. "\n"
    end

    return at .. NAMES[severity] .. " " .. module .. ": " .. message .. "\n"
end

local record StderrSink is Sink
    function emit(exclusive self, severity: Severity, module: string, line: integer, message: string): nil
        local _ = io.stderr:write(renderline(severity, module, line, message, nil))
    end

    close: @nosuspend function(takes self: StderrSink): nil
end

function StderrSink.close(takes self: StderrSink): nil
    local _ = @unsafe own.release(self)
end

local record CallbackSink is Sink
    @private callback: SinkCallback

    function emit(exclusive self, severity: Severity, module: string, line: integer, message: string): nil
        self.callback(severity, module, line, message)
    end

    close: @nosuspend function(takes self: CallbackSink): nil
end

function CallbackSink.close(takes self: CallbackSink): nil
    local _ = @unsafe own.release(self)
end

local record WriterSink is Sink
    @private destination: affine(io.Writer)
    @private formatter: Formatter?

    function emit(exclusive self, severity: Severity, module: string, line: integer, message: string): nil
        local _ = self.destination:write(renderline(severity, module, line, message, self.formatter))
    end

    close: @nosuspend function(takes self: WriterSink): nil
end

function WriterSink.close(takes self: WriterSink): nil
    self.destination:close()
end

target = @unsafe own.release(StderrSink())

--- Emits a line. Lowering calls it behind a level test.
--- @internal
export function emit(severity: integer, module: string, line: integer, message: string): any
    target:emit(severity, module, line, message)
end

local function flagsfor(name: string): {boolean}
    local flags = scoped[name]
    if not flags then
        flags = {false, false, false, false,}
        seton(flags, overrides[name] or threshold)
        scoped[name] = flags
    end

    return flags
end

--- The view a generated chunk binds once, so a site carries only severity and line.
---
--- Generated code calls this in a module's prologue. Nothing written by hand needs
--- it: a severity call in statement position is already lowered to a test against
--- the view's `on` array around a direct `emit`.
---
--- @param module the module name its lines carry
--- @return the view, whose `on` a site indexes and whose `emit` it calls
--- @internal
export function formodule(module: string): any
    local view = views[module]
    if not view then
        view = {
            on = flagsfor(module),
            emit = function(severity: integer, line: integer, message: string): any
                return emit(severity, module, line, message)
            end,
        }
        views[module] = view
    end

    return view
end

local function rank(level: Level): integer
    local index = INDEX[level]
    if index == nil then
        error("nupp: log has no level named " .. tostring(level), 3)
    end

    return index
end

-- `?` is the debug directive: the argument beside it is rendered through its own
-- `debug` method and the directive becomes an ordinary `%s`. Everything else is
-- handed to `string.format` unchanged.
local function render(message: string, ...: any): string
    local count = select("#", ...)
    if count == 0 then
        return message
    end
    local values = {...}
    if message:find("?", 1, true) then
        local pieces = {}
        local index = 1
        local arg: integer = 0
        while index <= #message do
            local percent = message:find("%", index, true)
            if not percent then
                pieces[#pieces + 1] = message:sub(index)
                break
            end
            pieces[#pieces + 1] = message:sub(index, percent - 1)
            if message:sub(percent + 1, percent + 1) == "%" then
                pieces[#pieces + 1] = "%%"
                index = percent + 2
            else
                local finish = percent + 1
                local current = message:sub(finish, finish)
                while current ~= "" and ("-+ #0"):find(current, 1, true) do
                    finish = finish + 1
                    current = message:sub(finish, finish)
                end
                while current:find("[0-9]") do
                    finish = finish + 1
                    current = message:sub(finish, finish)
                end
                if current == "." then
                    finish = finish + 1
                    current = message:sub(finish, finish)
                    while current:find("[0-9]") do
                        finish = finish + 1
                        current = message:sub(finish, finish)
                    end
                end
                arg = arg + 1
                if current == "?" then
                    values[arg] = values[arg]:debug()
                    current = "s"
                end
                pieces[#pieces + 1] = message:sub(percent, finish - 1) .. current
                index = finish + 1
            end
        end
        message = table.concat(pieces)
    end

    return string.format(message, unpack(values, 1, count))
end

local NO_OP = function(): nil
end

local function writer(severity: integer): any
    return function(self: any, message: string, ...: any): any
        return emit(severity, self.name, 0, render(message, ...))
    end
end

local WRITE = {writer(1), writer(2), writer(3), writer(4),}

local function admits(self: any, level: Level): boolean
    local index = rank(level)

    return index > 0 and self.on[index] or false
end

local function restamp(logger: any): nil
    logger.error = logger.on[1] and WRITE[1] or NO_OP
    logger.warn = logger.on[2] and WRITE[2] or NO_OP
    logger.info = logger.on[3] and WRITE[3] or NO_OP
    logger.debug = logger.on[4] and WRITE[4] or NO_OP
end

local function restampall(): nil
    for _, logger in pairs(registry) do
        restamp(logger)
    end
end

--- A logger with a fixed name. Repeating a name returns the same logger.
---
--- Reach for one where the intrinsic cannot rewrite the call: a format built at run
--- time, a call in expression position, a subsystem that wants its own name on every
--- line. The methods are restamped whenever the level or the target moves, so a
--- filtered call reaches an empty function rather than a test.
---
--- ```nupp:fragment
--- const log = log.named("renderer")
--- log:info("loaded %d entities", count)
--- ```
---
--- @param name the name its lines carry
--- @return the logger
--- @raises string when name is not a string
export function named(name: string): Logger
    if type(name) ~= "string" then
        error("nupp: log name must be a string", 2)
    end
    local logger = registry[name]
    if not logger then
        logger = {name = name, on = flagsfor(name), enabled = admits,}
        restamp(logger)
        registry[name] = logger
    end

    return logger as Logger
end

--- The global threshold.
---
--- @return the level in force for every module without an override
export function level(): Level
    return (NAMES[threshold] or "off") as Level
end

--- Moves the global threshold. Each level admits itself and everything above it.
---
--- @param next the new threshold
--- @return the threshold this call replaced
--- @raises string when next is unknown
export function setlevel(next: Level): Level
    local previous = (NAMES[threshold] or "off") as Level
    admit(rank(next))
    restampall()

    return previous
end

local function checkname(name: string): string
    if type(name) ~= "string" then
        error("nupp: log module name must be a string", 3)
    end

    return name
end

--- The effective threshold for a specific module or named logger: its override, or
--- the global threshold when it has none.
---
--- @param name the exact module or logger name
--- @return the level in force for that name
--- @raises string when name is not a string
export function modulelevel(name: string): Level
    return (NAMES[overrides[checkname(name)] or threshold] or "off") as Level
end

--- Overrides the threshold for a specific module or named logger.
---
--- A name without an override inherits the global threshold. Passing `"inherit"`
--- removes an override. Existing and future generated module views observe the
--- change while retaining the call-site guard that avoids evaluating filtered
--- arguments.
---
--- @param name the exact module or logger name
--- @param next the new threshold, or `"inherit"`
--- @return the effective threshold this call replaced
--- @raises string when name is not a string or next is unknown
export function setmodulelevel(name: string, next: LevelOverride): Level
    local previous = modulelevel(checkname(name))
    if next == "inherit" then
        overrides[name] = nil
        seton(flagsfor(name), threshold)
    else
        local index = rank(next)
        overrides[name] = index
        seton(flagsfor(name), index)
    end
    local logger = registry[name]
    if logger then
        restamp(logger)
    end

    return previous
end

--- Whether a level is currently admitted.
---
--- @param at the level to test
--- @return whether a call at that level would emit
export function enabled(at: Level): boolean
    local index = rank(at)

    return index > 0 and on[index] or false
end

--- Creates a sink that calls `handler` for each message.
--- @param handler the callback receiving each message
--- @return the sink, owned by the caller
export function callback(handler: SinkCallback): affine(Sink)
    if type(handler) ~= "function" then
        error("nupp: log callback must be a function", 2)
    end
    return CallbackSink(callback = handler)
end

--- Creates a sink that renders messages to an owned writer.
--- @param destination the writer to take; any resource it borrows must outlive the sink
--- @param formatter the line formatter, or nil for the default
--- @return the sink, owned by the caller
export function writersink(takes destination: io.Writer, formatter: Formatter?): affine(Sink)
    if formatter ~= nil and type(formatter) ~= "function" then
        error("nupp: log formatter must be a function", 2)
    end
    return WriterSink(destination = destination, formatter = formatter)
end

--- Replaces the sink, transferring ownership of both sinks.
---
--- @param next the sink to install
--- @return the previous sink, owned by the caller
export function setsink(takes next: Sink): affine(Sink)
    local kind = type(next)
    if (kind ~= "table" and kind ~= "userdata" and kind ~= "cdata")
        or type((next as any).emit) ~= "function"
        or type((next as any).close) ~= "function"
    then
        error("nupp: log sink must implement emit and close", 2)
    end
    local previous = target
    target = @unsafe own.release(next)
    restampall()

    return @unsafe own.adopt<affine(Sink)>(previous)
end

--- The stamp a line written now would carry.
---
--- @return the rendered timestamp, or "" when stamping is off
export function timestamp(): string
    return stamp()
end

--- The timestamp format in force.
---
--- @return the `os.date` format, or "" when stamping is off
export function timestampformat(): string
    return datefmt
end

--- Replaces the timestamp format. An empty format stops stamping.
---
--- @param next the `os.date` format
--- @return the format this call replaced
--- @raises string when next is not a string
export function settimestampformat(next: string): string
    if type(next) ~= "string" then
        error("nupp: log timestamp format must be a string", 2)
    end
    local previous = datefmt
    datefmt = next
    lasttick = -1

    return previous
end

--- The name for a sink's numeric level.
---
--- @param severity the numeric level a sink received
--- @return the level's name
export function levelname(severity: integer): Level
    return (NAMES[severity] or "off") as Level
end

local output = formodule("warn")
local warnon = true

local function checkarg(value: any, index: integer): string
    if type(value) == "string" then
        return value
    elseif type(value) == "number" then
        return tostring(value)
    end
    error(("bad argument #%d to 'warn' (string expected, got %s)"):format(index, type(value)), 3)
end

-- Joins messages and emits a warning, or applies a single control string.
local function warning(message: string, ...: string): nil
    local parts = {checkarg(message, 1)}
    for index = 1, select("#", ...) do
        parts[index + 1] = checkarg(select(index, ...), index + 1)
    end
    if #parts == 1 and parts[1]:sub(1, 1) == "@" then
        if parts[1] == "@on" then
            warnon = true
        elseif parts[1] == "@off" then
            warnon = false
        end
        return
    end
    if warnon then
        output.emit(2, 0, table.concat(parts))
    end
end

rawset(_G, "warn", warning)

--- Lua's `warn`, which the `warn` module publishes.
--- @internal
export const warner = warning

-- Written out rather than built by a closure factory, because each one carries the
-- format pack its own directives are checked against. A statement call with a literal
-- format is rewritten to a test around `emit` and never reaches these. Bound last:
-- an exported function would shadow Lua's `error` for the whole file.

local function logerror<F is string>(fmt: F, ...: unpackof __NuppFormatArguments(F, types.Debug)): nil
    if not on[1] then
        return
    end
    emit(1, "?", 0, render(fmt as string, ...))
end

local function logwarn<F is string>(fmt: F, ...: unpackof __NuppFormatArguments(F, types.Debug)): nil
    if not on[2] then
        return
    end
    emit(2, "?", 0, render(fmt as string, ...))
end

local function loginfo<F is string>(fmt: F, ...: unpackof __NuppFormatArguments(F, types.Debug)): nil
    if not on[3] then
        return
    end
    emit(3, "?", 0, render(fmt as string, ...))
end

local function logdebug<F is string>(fmt: F, ...: unpackof __NuppFormatArguments(F, types.Debug)): nil
    if not on[4] then
        return
    end
    emit(4, "?", 0, render(fmt as string, ...))
end

--- Logs at error. Accepts `string.format` directives.
export const error: function<F is string>(fmt: F, ...: unpackof __NuppFormatArguments(F, types.Debug)): nil = logerror

--- Logs at warn. Accepts `string.format` directives.
export const warn: function<F is string>(fmt: F, ...: unpackof __NuppFormatArguments(F, types.Debug)): nil = logwarn

--- Logs at info. Accepts `string.format` directives.
export const info: function<F is string>(fmt: F, ...: unpackof __NuppFormatArguments(F, types.Debug)): nil = loginfo

--- Logs at debug. Accepts `string.format` directives.
export const debug: function<F is string>(fmt: F, ...: unpackof __NuppFormatArguments(F, types.Debug)): nil = logdebug
